Q: How to delete a line in a string A: <p>A simple approach would be to do the following:</p>
<ol>
<li>Split the string using <code>lines()</code>.</li>
<li>Convert to a <code>Vec</code> using <code>collect()</code>;</li>
<li>Remove the line (which is an element in the vector) by index using <code>remove()</code>.</li>
<li>Convert back to string using <code>join()</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p>This is shown below:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>fn main() {
    let mut string = String::from(&quot;Hello\nWorld&quot;);

    let line_idx_to_remove = 1;

    let mut lines: Vec&lt;_&gt; = string.lines().collect();
    lines.remove(line_idx_to_remove);
    string = lines.join(&quot;\n&quot;);

    println!(&quot;{}&quot;, string);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Hello
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Notes:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Your <code>string</code> should be marked mutable (<code>mut</code>) if you want to modify it.</li>
<li>In your real code you should verify <code>line_idx_to_remove</code> is a valid index.</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&amp;mode=debug&amp;edition=2024&amp;gist=260ef5e751714ed6a81981c736f0c186" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 1 - Rust Playground</a></p>
<p>Alternatively, you can use the following approach which is perhaps more idiomatic (but maybe a little less straightforward):</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>fn main() {
    let string = String::from(&quot;Hello\nWorld&quot;);

    let line_idx_to_remove = 1;

    let string = string
        .lines()
        .enumerate()
        .filter(|(i, _)| *i != line_idx_to_remove)
        .map(|(_, line)| line)
        .collect::&lt;Vec&lt;_&gt;&gt;()
        .join(&quot;\n&quot;);

    println!(&quot;{}&quot;, string);
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&amp;mode=debug&amp;edition=2024&amp;gist=fd477706120d7c1012a1859307d34259" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Live demo 2 - Rust Playground</a></p>

Q: Sum column data based on matching ID php query A: <p>This is how I'd write this PHP script.</p>
<pre><code>$file = fopen($cfile,&quot;r&quot;);
while (($data = fgetcsv($file, escape: &quot;\\&quot;)) !== FALSE) {
  $lp_filename = $data[0];
  if($lp_filename != &quot;Loan Number&quot;){
    $lp_filename_map[$lp_filename] = true;
  }
}
fclose($file);

$params = array_keys($lp_filename_map);
$placeholders = join(',', array_fill(0, count($params), '?'));

$sql = &quot;
    SELECT id, SUM(amount) AS amount
    FROM commission
    WHERE lp_filename IN ({$placeholders})
    GROUP BY id&quot;;
$result = mysqli_execute_query($dbc, $sql, $params);
foreach ($result as $row) {
  print &quot;{$row['id']},{$row['amount']}\n&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I tested with PHP 8.5.7 and MySQL 9.7.0. The output is as follows:</p>
<pre><code>2404,300
2424,500
3150,550
2501,750
</code></pre>
<p>This solution has advantages:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>It runs only one SQL query, which collects all the sums of amounts by aggregation.</p>
</li>
<li><p>It uses query parameters, instead of <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/601524/20860">interpolating unsafe content into the SQL query</a>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>I don't know how large your input CSV is, so I don't know if this will result in an <code>IN()</code> predicate with thousands of values. But I suppose that's still better than running thousands of individual SQL queries.</p>
<p>Here is the test data I used:</p>
<pre><code>create table commission (
  id int,
  lp_filename text,
  amount int
);

insert into commission values
(2404,'filename1',100),
(2404,'filename2',200),
(2424,'filename3',500),
(3150,'filename4',550),
(2501,'filename5',600),
(2501,'filename6',150);
</code></pre>
<p>And cfile.csv:</p>
<pre><code>Loan Number
filename1
filename2
filename3
filename4
filename5
filename6
</code></pre>
